Third-party code
----------------
This file records all of the external code that the project has taken or
adapted, with its origin, its licence and the file in which it lives. To take
code from legendary or from Heroic Games Launcher (the two are GPLv3) agrees
with this licence, but you must keep their copyright headers and record them
here.

Until now no third-party code has been copied or adapted: there is no external
copyright header to keep.

There is knowledge taken from external projects. It is recorded here for
honesty, although no licence makes that necessary:

  The GOG connector (crates/connectors/src/gog/)
  The authorisation flow and the current endpoints were found in Heroic Games
  Launcher and gogdl, the two GPLv3, and were examined by hand on 2026-08-14:
    - https://github.com/Heroic-Games-Launcher/HeroicGamesLauncher
      Copyright (c) Heroic Games Launcher contributors
    - https://github.com/Heroic-Games-Launcher/heroic-gogdl
      Copyright (c) 2021-2026 Paweł Lidwin and contributors
  From there comes the fact that the library is no longer read from
  embed.gog.com but from galaxy-library.gog.com. No code has been copied: the
  implementation belongs to this project and is in Rust.

  The client credentials of GOG Galaxy
  GOG does not let you register third-party applications, thus the only client
  that its authorisation server accepts is the client of GOG Galaxy. That pair
  has been published in gogdl for years. This program does **not** carry it
  inside: the user supplies it when they connect their account, and it is kept
  in the store of secrets, as each other credential is.

  The Epic connector (crates/connectors/src/epic/)
  Epic publishes no library API: what exists is the private API of its own
  launcher. The authorisation flow and the current endpoints were found in
  legendary, GPLv3, and were examined by hand on 2026-08-15:
    - https://github.com/legendary-gl/legendary
      Copyright (c) derrod and the contributors of legendary
  From there come three facts that no documentation gives: that the
  authorisation code comes in the body of `id/api/redirect` and not in a
  redirect, that you must ask for the token with `token_type=eg1`, and that the
  `ue` namespace contains Unreal Engine assets and not games. No code has been
  copied: the implementation belongs to this project and is in Rust.

  The client credentials of the Epic launcher
  As with GOG, Epic does not let you register third-party applications and the
  only client that its authorisation server accepts is the client of its
  launcher. That pair is published in legendary. This program does **not** carry
  it inside: the user supplies it when they connect their account.

About the stores
----------------
This project has no relation to Valve, GOG, CD PROJEKT or Epic Games. The
connectors only read, they operate with the credentials of the user and on the
machine of the user, and they give the data of no store to other persons.

The project also has no relation to IsThereAnyDeal. The prices come from its
public API with documentation, with the key of the user, from the machine of the
user and only for the games of their wishlist. No code of theirs has been taken:
there is none to take, because the API has documentation and no reference
implementation was necessary.
